body:
I using cJson ( C ) into an embedded project to parse a JSON object.

After an intense debugging session I found a problem. The memory requested by the cJSON object isn't released completely. I found this problem printing on UART the free heap size.

This is my code:
```c
cJSON    *jsonObject;
cJSON    *jsonItem;
uint8_t  protocolVersion;
char     machineId[DATA_PROTOCOL_PROPERTY_MACHINE_ID_MAX_LEN];

/* Free Heap: 22688 Byte */
jsonObject = cJSON_CreateObject();
/* Free Heap: 22640 Byte. cJSON object uses 48 Byte */

jsonObject = cJSON_Parse( jsonMessage );

jsonItem = cJSON_GetObjectItem( jsonObject, "ProtocolVerison" );
protocolVersion = (uint8_t)jsonItem->valueint;

jsonItem = cJSON_GetObjectItem( jsonObject, "MachineID" );
strncpy( machineId, jsonItem->valuestring, SDK_DATA_PROTOCOL_PROPERTY_MACHINE_ID_MAX_LEN );

/* Free Heap: 22152 Byte. cJSON object occupies 488 Byte */
cJSON_Delete( jsonObject );
/* Free Heap: 22640 Byte. cJSON object deleted */

```

The difference between the free heap memory at start (22688 byte) and the free heap at the end ( 22640 byte ) is 48 byte equal to the cJSON object before start parsing.

This 48 byte, after several loop bring the system into a memory leak ( my application consume the entire heap).

comments.body:
Yes, there is a memory leak in your code. Although it is not created by cJSON.

```c
cJSON    *jsonObject;
cJSON    *jsonItem;
uint8_t  protocolVersion;
char     machineId[SDK_DATA_PROTOCOL_PROPERTY_MACHINE_ID_MAX_LEN];

/* This code mallocs your 48 bytes */
jsonObject = cJSON_CreateObject(); /* this line is unnecessary and creates the memory leak */

/* here you are overwriting your pointer to the 48 bytes by overwriting it with the newly parsed object, thereby creating a memory leak */
jsonObject = cJSON_Parse( jsonMessage );
/* please check if jsonObject is NULL and abort if it is */

jsonItem = cJSON_GetObjectItem( jsonObject, "ProtocolVerison" );
/* same here, before using jsonItem, check if it is NULL */
/* Also check the type, you can check if it is a number using cJSON_IsNumber(jsonItem) */
protocolVersion = (uint8_t)jsonItem->valueint;

jsonItem = cJSON_GetObjectItem( jsonObject, "MachineID" );
/* same here, check if jsonItem is NULL and check if it is a string using cJSON_IsString(jsonItem) */
strncpy( machineId, jsonItem->valuestring, SDK_DATA_PROTOCOL_PROPERTY_MACHINE_ID_MAX_LEN ); /* you could also use sizeof(machineId) */

/* This only frees what has been parsed by cJSON_Parse, your 48 bytes have already been lost earlier */
cJSON_Delete( jsonObject );
```
